For $\mathbf F=P\,\mathbf i+Q\,\mathbf j$, the **scalar curl** is $Q_x-P_y$. Hold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative. Both cross-partials require the product rule and the chain rule; retain both terms from each product.

$$
\begin{aligned}
Q_x&=\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(2 x e^{x y} + y^{2}\right)\\
&=2 x y e^{x y} + 2 e^{x y}\\
P_y&=\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(x^{2} + 2 y \ln{\left(x + y \right)}\right)\\
&=\frac{2 y}{x + y} + 2 \ln{\left(x + y \right)}\\
Q_x-P_y&=\left(2 x y e^{x y} + 2 e^{x y}\right)-\left(\frac{2 y}{x + y} + 2 \ln{\left(x + y \right)}\right)\\
&=2 x y e^{x y} - \frac{2 y}{x + y} + 2 e^{x y} - 2 \ln{\left(x + y \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate first, then substitute the point:

$$
\begin{aligned}
(Q_x-P_y)(2,1)&=\left(6 e^{2}\right)-\left(\frac{2}{3} + 2 \ln{\left(3 \right)}\right)\\
&=- 2 \ln{\left(3 \right)} - \frac{2}{3} + 6 e^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

For $\mathbf F=P\,\mathbf i+Q\,\mathbf j$, the **scalar curl** is $Q_x-P_y$. Hold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative. Both cross-partials require the product rule and the chain rule; retain both terms from each product.

$$
\begin{aligned}
Q_x&=\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(2 x \ln{\left(x + y \right)}\right)\\
&=\frac{2 x}{x + y} + 2 \ln{\left(x + y \right)}\\
P_y&=\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(- 2 y e^{x y}\right)\\
&=- 2 x y e^{x y} - 2 e^{x y}\\
Q_x-P_y&=\left(\frac{2 x}{x + y} + 2 \ln{\left(x + y \right)}\right)-\left(- 2 x y e^{x y} - 2 e^{x y}\right)\\
&=2 x y e^{x y} + \frac{2 x}{x + y} + 2 e^{x y} + 2 \ln{\left(x + y \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate first, then substitute the point:

$$
\begin{aligned}
(Q_x-P_y)(-1,3)&=\left(-1 + 2 \ln{\left(2 \right)}\right)-\left(\frac{4}{e^{3}}\right)\\
&=-1 - \frac{4}{e^{3}} + 2 \ln{\left(2 \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$
